<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Cleartext HTTP is permitted.

  `opds-catalog` and `network-share` are about servers a reader runs at home, and a Synology
  or a Raspberry Pi on the shelf usually answers over plain HTTP on a `.local` name or a
  private address. Blocked, every self-hosted catalogue is unreachable with no way to say
  why.

  iOS expresses this narrowly: `NSAllowsLocalNetworking` permits insecure loads to local
  names and private ranges and nothing else. Android has no equivalent — a network security
  config can name a host or an IP literal, but not a range — so the exception here is wider
  than the one on iOS, and that asymmetry is deliberate rather than an oversight.

  What keeps it honest is the app, in two places rather than one.

  The address field completes a missing scheme to `https`, never `http`, so reaching a
  catalogue over cleartext takes a reader typing `http://`. That covers what a reader types
  and nothing else — and a catalogue chooses every address fetched after the first one.

  So `OpdsOrigin` covers the rest: a resolved feed href that steps down from the source's own
  `https` to `http` is refused outright, and one that leaves the source's origin at all is
  fetched without the credential. The permission below stays wide because Android cannot
  express "every private range"; what may actually be reached over cleartext is decided in
  code, against the origin the reader configured.
-->
<network-security-config xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">
    <!--
      Lint's advice is right in general and wrong for this app: an app that only ever talks
      to services it owns should refuse cleartext, and this one exists to talk to servers
      its readers own. Suppressed at the line rather than project-wide, so the next
      cleartext config somebody adds is still flagged.
    -->
    <base-config
        cleartextTrafficPermitted="true"
        tools:ignore="InsecureBaseConfiguration">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
